    "created_at": "2016-08-15T03:00:22Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-08-15T03:00:22Z",
    "body": "Just to reiterate that something must be done: By not setting the insertion point, document.write becomes \"destructive\" and hence calls [document.open](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/#dom-document-open), but since the script nesting level is nonzero that becomes a no-op. This causes document.write to continue on talking about inserting characters at an insertion point it intends to be defined, but is in fact undefined.\n\nSo to specify a general constraint implicit in parsing HTML as it stands today: When incrementing the script nesting level, either define the insertion point or increment the ignore destructive writes counter/ignore opens during unload counter.\n\nI think the extra flag could be OK. In my experiments implementing so far, maybe the ignore destructive writes counter is enough, but a week of fuzzing may change my mind about that. Should we try relying on ignore destructive writes and let me get back to you?\n",
